    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d529">

                <pb facs="0547" n="535" xml:id="pg535"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">360F.115 Int. Tel. and Tel. Corp./10–545:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-11-16T16:00:00-05:00">November 16,
                            1945—4 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2352. Reurtel 3456 October 5. Dept advised of Soviet intention to remove by
                    December 1 equipment in vacuum tube plant at Vrchlabi and Ferdinand Schachardt
                    telephone set factory at Bruntal presumably as war “trophies”. Dept requests you
                    strongly protest this action as contrary to Potsdam Agreement<anchor n="75"
                        xml:id="d529-f1"/> under Article IV, paragraphs 1, 5, 8 and 9 which specify
                    that only German external assets available for Soviet reparations are those
                    located in Finland, Hungary, Rumania, Bulgaria and Soviet occupied zone of
                        Reich.<anchor n="76" xml:id="d529-f2"/> Furthermore, this Government cannot
                    accept apparent Soviet position that this equipment constitutes war booty and as
                    such subject to removal by Soviet Government in violation of above paragraphs of
                    Potsdam Agreement. This equipment clearly capable of use for civilian peacetime
                    production without reconversion. Neither does US Government recognize that
                    origin of equipment (Urtel paragraph 2) proves such equipment not property in
                    which US nationals may have ownership interests.</p>
                <p>Sent to Moscow repeated to Prague.<anchor n="77" xml:id="d529-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d529-f1" n="75" xml:id="d529-f1-content">
                <p>See Report on the Tripartite Conference of Berlin, released to the press on
                    August 2, 1945, <ref target="frus1945Berlinv02#pg_1499">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Conference of Berlin
                        (The Potsdam Conference), 1945, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p.
                        1499</ref>, or Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>,
                    August 5, 1945, p. 153.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d529-f2" n="76" xml:id="d529-f2-content">
                <p>For documentation regarding German reparations and restitution, see <ref
                        target="frus1945v03#pg_1169">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, pp.
                        1169</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d529-f3" n="77" xml:id="d529-f3-content">
                <p>Repeated to Prague as telegram 388.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
